# Local Claude Code state is machine-specific and stays ignored. The skills
# directory does not: `.claude/skills/` is project tooling that encodes
# governance rules, so it is institutional knowledge under Law VII and belongs
# in the repository. The `/*` form is required — git will not descend into an
# ignored directory, so `.claude/` followed by a negation would not work.
.claude/*

!.claude/skills/

.DS_Store

Thumbs.db

reports/generated/

# The market data store. ADR-013 rule 4, and this entry is the enforcement
# rather than a convention someone has to remember.
#
# Git retains every version of every file permanently by design, so a
# daily-refreshed price series makes repository growth unbounded with no
# prunable path — and append-only history means the mistake cannot be undone
# later. A vendor licence may also restrict retention independently of
# visibility, and removing data from a git history is effectively impossible
# where removing a directory is not.
#
# What is kept instead, and where: `artifacts/`, tracked. ADR-014 rules 2 and 6.
# The fetch record for every stored version carries that version's content hash,
# its path inside this store, and the immutable source key it was filed under —
# so the two can be checked against each other rather than assumed to agree.
# `orphan_versions()` reports any stored version no record accounts for, and
# `test_no_stored_version_in_this_repository_is_unrecorded` runs it.
#
# **That check is machine-local, and this comment says so rather than implying
# otherwise.** This entry keeps `data/market/` out of every clone, so on a machine
# that has not fetched, the check walks an empty store and passes with nothing
# examined. It is real only where the bytes are. An independent pass found the
# first version of this paragraph claiming the check without that qualification —
# which is the same gap ADR-014 rule 9 exists to close, inside rule 9's own
# implementation. See docs/HANDOFF.md, "Rule 7's Orphan Check Is Vacuous On Every
# Machine But The One That Fetched".
#
# This paragraph replaces one that read "Nothing is lost by this ... the record
# of what was obtained is signed and permanent whether or not the bytes are
# under version control." **That was false for the whole time it stood here** —
# no fetch record had ever been written to disk, which is F-002. It is not
# restored in a milder wording, because the comparison it made was the wrong
# one: at the daily cadence Belay actually fetches, the tracked record is larger
# than the ignored payload it describes. The two arguments above stand on their
# own and do not need this one.
#
# Added before the first byte landed, deliberately. Git history cannot be pruned
# afterwards.
data/market/

# The cloned upstream database. Finding 14, and a separate entry from the store
# above on purpose.
#
# `data/market/` holds *fetched series* — keyed by source and symbol, versioned
# when the vendor restates. This holds a copy of the vendor's whole database. If
# the two shared a directory, rule 4's version count would stop meaning "the
# number of times the vendor's answer changed" and start counting clone
# refreshes as well.
#
# Multi-gigabyte and reconstructible by re-cloning, so it is a cache rather than
# a record. Nothing signed depends on it: the fetch record carries the content
# hash of the bytes, and those bytes live in the store.
#
# Added before the clone was started, for the same reason the entry above was.
data/upstream/

logs/

temp/

.cache/

# A safety net, not a policy: credential and local-database shapes that must
# never be committed from any contributor's machine, whatever tools they use.
# Appended at the end on purpose: this file is cited by line number in ADRs,
# code and tests, and an insertion higher up would move every one of them.
.env.*
*.pem
*.key
*.p12
*.pfx
*.pickle
*.pkl
*.sqlite
*.sqlite3
*.db
credentials*
secrets*
.tokens/
